From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: mm/madvise: notify userfaultfd when installing guard regions
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:49:58 -0800

MADV_GUARD_INSTALL can discard the contents of an anonymous range before
installing guard markers. A userfaultfd manager with MISSING registration
does not receive a remove event. If it retains a copy of the discarded
contents, it can supply that old data when the guard is removed and the
next access faults, instead of treating the range as discarded. The same
operation also drops uffd-wp and RWP markers without telling a manager
that it might need to adjust its write tracking.

Send UFFD_EVENT_REMOVE before installing the guards, as MADV_DONTNEED
does. If the event drops mmap_lock, reacquire it and look up and validate
the VMA again before modifying it. Limit the current operation to the
revalidated VMA; madvise_walk_vmas() handles any remaining range.

Tal Zussman previously proposed this notification [1]. This version is
based on the tree containing Lorenzo Stoakes's marker-dropping fix [2].
The stale-data path has not yet been reproduced by this review; this
patch is for A/B testing with a manager that retains old page contents.

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/20261111-uffd-state-fixes-v1-11-00713d368976@columbia.edu [1]
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/ofd747zeh26u3rba42fsdryoe2mwdhi6r4cjvrmjn25tps4ar4@ubcwdhamdyui [2]
Fixes: 662df3e5c376 ("mm: madvise: implement lightweight guard page mechanism")
Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Reported-by: n3rd <18670333770@163.com>
Cc: Tal Zussman <tz2294@columbia.edu>
Cc: Lorenzo Stoakes <ljs@kernel.org>
Cc: David Hildenbrand <david@kernel.org>
Cc: Peter Xu <peterx@redhat.com>
Cc: Axel Rasmussen <axelrasmussen@google.com>

 mm/madvise.c |   22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

--- a/mm/madvise.c~mm-madvise-notify-userfaultfd-when-installing-guard-regions
+++ a/mm/madvise.c
@@ -1164,6 +1164,28 @@ static long madvise_guard_install(struct
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	/*
+	 * Installing guards can discard pages. Tell a userfaultfd manager
+	 * before doing so, as MADV_DONTNEED does. Recheck the VMA if the
+	 * event drops the mmap lock.
+	 */
+	if (!userfaultfd_remove(vma, range->start, range->end)) {
+		mark_mmap_lock_dropped(madv_behavior);
+		mmap_read_lock(madv_behavior->mm);
+		madv_behavior->vma = vma = vma_lookup(madv_behavior->mm,
+						    range->start);
+		if (!vma)
+			return -ENOMEM;
+		if (!is_valid_guard_vma(vma, /* allow_locked = */false))
+			return -EINVAL;
+		/*
+		 * If the VMA was split, the next madvise_walk_vmas()
+		 * iteration handles the rest of the range.
+		 */
+		if (range->end > vma->vm_end)
+			range->end = vma->vm_end;
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * Set atomically under read lock. All pertinent readers will need to
 	 * acquire an mmap/VMA write lock to read it. All remaining readers may
 	 * or may not see the flag set, but we don't care.
